---
import Legal from '../../components/Legal.astro';
import { legalHref } from '../../i18n';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

// The English version of /privacidad: keep both saying the same.
---

<Legal lang="en" page="privacy" title="Privacy policy" updated="2026-10-05">
  <div class="summary">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Without an account, your diary never leaves your device: diaryo sends it nowhere.</li>
      <li>
        With an account, we keep your email and your diary <strong>encrypted on your device</strong>:
        we can't read it.
      </li>
      <li>No ads, no tracking, and we don't sell or share your data with anyone.</li>
      <li>You can download your data and delete your account in Settings, whenever you like.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is made and run by {SITE.owner}, as an individual. For anything about your data,
    write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contact}`}>{SITE.contact}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Using diaryo without an account</h2>
  <p>
    The app keeps the diary on your device: in the desktop app, on your computer (and its daily
    backups, in the folder you choose); in the web app, in your browser's storage. It isn't sent
    to any server. The app has no ads and no analytics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now and then the desktop app checks for a new version by asking GitHub, which sees your IP
    address as with any download.
  </p>

  <h2>If you create an account (the cloud)</h2>
  <p>The cloud is optional, to sync your diary between your devices. For that we keep:</p>
  <table style="--how-long: 'How long'">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>What</th><th>What for</th><th>How long</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Your email</td>
          <td>
            To identify your account, let you sign in, and send you the emails to confirm your
            address or choose a new password. We don't send you advertising.
          </td>
          <td>Until you delete the account</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The account's password</td>
          <td>So only you can sign in. It is stored transformed (a hash), never readable.</td>
          <td>Until you delete the account</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your diary, encrypted</td>
          <td>
            To sync it. Each page, element, image and font is encrypted on your device before it
            leaves, with a key only you have (it comes from your diary password). The server keeps
            unreadable data, with ids that don't say what they are, the time of each change and its
            size.
          </td>
          <td>Until you delete it or the account</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Account details</td>
          <td>To provide the service: the plan, the space you use, and when it was created and changed.</td>
          <td>Until you delete the account</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Server logs</td>
          <td>
            Security and fixing problems: each request to the server, with the IP address, the time
            and what was asked for (not its content).
          </td>
          <td>5 days</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>We don't know your diary password or your recovery code</strong>, so we can't read your
    diary, nor recover it if you lose both. What is on your devices stays there.
  </p>
  <h3>Signing in with Google</h3>
  <p>
    Google confirms your email and an id of your Google account to us, and nothing else: we don't
    keep your name or picture, nor ask for access to any other data. diaryo's use of information
    received from Google APIs adheres to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>
  <h3>Why we may process it</h3>
  <p>
    To provide the service you ask for when you create the account (article 6.1.b of the GDPR).
    The server logs, for the legitimate interest in keeping it secure (article 6.1.f).
  </p>

  <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
  <p>Only the providers we need, who process the data on our behalf and under contract:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Hetzner Online GmbH</strong> (Germany): the server where the account and the encrypted
      diary are kept, in Nuremberg.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resend</strong> (United States): sends the confirmation and password emails, so it
      receives your email address and the content of those emails. The transfer outside the EU has
      the safeguards the GDPR requires (the European Commission's standard contractual clauses).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google</strong>, only if you choose to sign in with Google: it checks it's you, under
      its own privacy policy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We don't sell or share your data with anyone else, unless a law requires it.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website counts visits and downloads with Umami, hosted on our own server, without cookies
    and without keeping anything that identifies you: only totals (which pages are seen, from which
    country, browser or website people arrive). The installer downloads come from GitHub.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither the website nor the app use cookies. The app keeps the account's session and your
    settings on your device, because it couldn't work without them.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can access your data, correct it, delete it, take it with you, restrict its use or object to
    it. The usual things are in the app, in <strong>Settings → Account and cloud</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Download your data</strong>: what the server keeps about your account.</li>
    <li><strong>Save a copy</strong> (in the menu): your whole diary, readable, in one file.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delete the account</strong>: the account and all your diary in the cloud are deleted
      at once. We make no backups of the server, so nothing is left.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For anything else, write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contact}`}>{SITE.contact}</a>; we answer
    within a month. If you think we don't handle your data well, you can complain to the
    <a href="https://www.aepd.es/en">Spanish Data Protection Agency</a> (or the one in your
    country).
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Everything travels over HTTPS, and your diary is end-to-end encrypted (AES-256-GCM, with the
    key derived from your diary password with PBKDF2). diaryo's code is public: anyone can check how
    it works.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    To create an account you must be at least 14 (or the age your country's law asks, if higher).
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If anything in this policy changes, you'll see it here with its date; if it is important and
    you have an account, we'll tell you by email first. See also the <a
      href={legalHref('en', 'terms')}>terms</a
    >.
  </p>
</Legal>
